N1  - Using first-half foul sequences from 365 NCAA games, Anderson and Pierce found that the team with fewer accumulated fouls was more likely to receive the next call after accounting for score and venue. This supplies a direct precedent for examining foul history and suggests testing cumulative foul differential alongside WMI’s recent-history measures. Its low explanatory power, college sample, and inability to observe true infractions limit causal interpretations and make an NBA possession-based extension useful.
N1  - Methods: Observational analysis of 365 NCAA men’s games in 2004–05: 272 conference home games and 93 neutral games. First-half fouls only to reduce intentional-fouling confounding. Binomial-independence comparison and logistic models of which team receives the next foul, controlling score/lead and conference; reports sensitivity to clustering by game.
N1  - Findings: More accumulated fouls on a team predicted a lower probability that the next foul was called against it. Home-game net-foul coefficient −0.1180 and marginal effect −0.0295; neutral-game coefficient −0.1469. Both models have pseudo-R² below .02. Leading and visiting teams were also more likely to receive the next foul.
N1  - Limits: Recorded calls do not reveal true infractions or officiating intent; changing aggression/strategy remains an alternative. NCAA first halves from one season do not establish NBA effects. This models next called foul conditional on a foul occurring, rather than the next possession’s foul probability.
N1  - Evidence: Manuscript PDF pp. 8–11: hypotheses and methods; 365 games, first-half restriction, logistic specifications.; Manuscript PDF pp. 11–13 and Tables 1–2 on PDF pp. 22–23: results.; Manuscript PDF pp. 13–17: discussion and player-behavior alternative.
