KW  - WMI Checkpoint 3
N1  - Gong analyzed 30,695 reviewed foul actions from 1,679 NBA games, using pandemic empty arenas to examine crowd effects and separating incorrect calls from missed calls. The study found no overall crowd-driven difference in treatment of home and away teams, although specific foul categories showed differences. I will use it to justify venue and foul-type controls and to explain why WMI’s recorded-call patterns cannot establish referee error across an entire game.
N1  - Methods: 30,695 foul actions from 1,679 qualifying NBA regular-season games, 2017–18 through 2020–21; excludes neutral bubble games. Separates incorrect calls from incorrect non-calls using league reviews. Linear probability models with home/fan interaction, team/referee and calendar fixed effects, game-clustered errors; compares crowds with empty arenas.
N1  - Findings: No evidence that crowd presence changes home-versus-away treatment overall in this close-game sample. Fans associated with a 2.3 percentage-point increase in missed offensive-foul calls, while home players had 2.4 percentage points more missed loose-ball fouls; subgroup findings do not generalize to all foul categories.
N1  - Limits: Close games and final two minutes/OT only; regular-season scope; no-fan games all from pandemic season and league review is the correctness benchmark. Observational pandemic comparison cannot fully isolate every simultaneous change. A non-significant interaction is not proof that crowds never matter.
N1  - Evidence: Published pp. 955–962: methodology, L2M sample, models, clustering, Table 3.; Published pp. 963–968: home/fan effects and interaction models.; Published pp. 969–971: discussion, conclusion, external-validity limits.
