N1  - Pelechrinis uses NBA Last Two Minute reports and Monte Carlo simulations to evaluate home, player, team, and racial patterns in officiating, finding aggregate home benefit and positive benefit for some players but no aggregate evidence of team or technical-foul racial bias. The study shows that call frequency alone cannot establish bias because actual violations, missed calls, and sample selection matter. I will use it to frame WMI as descriptive foul sequencing and to distinguish next-possession prediction from an accusation of incorrect officiating.
N1  - Methods: NBA L2M reports covering 2015–2022; Monte Carlo null distributions for net whistle gain using violation-specific CC/IC/INC base rates; 106 players with at least 100 calls/noncalls; separate analysis of 5,419 personal technical fouls with exposure minutes and referee/player race.
N1  - Findings: Combined-season home net whistle gain exceeded simulated expectation by 145.55 (Table 2, p < .01); regular-season estimate 107.7, p=.03; playoff estimate 47.86, p<.01. 2020–2022 combined-period test was nonsignificant (p=.49). Twelve players had positive nominally significant gain; binomial meta-test probability approximately .007. No aggregate evidence for negative player or team bias after that meta-test. Opposite-minus-same-race technical rate difference was .0022 per 48 minutes, simulation p=.33.
N1  - Limits: L2M is selected close-game final-two-minute evidence; do not generalize to every NBA possession. CNC inclusion changes over seasons (6.4/game in 2015 versus almost 14 in 2022), so the analysis excludes that inconsistent denominator. Small-category base rates can be noisy; meta-test assumes uncorrelated player tests; pandemic-period association cannot isolate crowd absence from other changes.
N1  - Evidence: PDF pp. 2–3: L2M selection, CNC problems, simulation design; PDF pp. 5–7: Table 2, player and technical-foul results; PDF pp. 7–9: interpretation and limitations
