N1  - Morgulev and colleagues analyze 250 potential offensive-foul collisions from an Israeli professional season using group comparisons and logistic regression. They find no statistically significant home, reputation, or size effect and identify the small, narrow sample as a limitation for studying sequential calls. This source will help me explain why WMI must report uncertainty and cannot treat an absence of significance or a pattern in a different foul category as definitive NBA evidence.
N1  - Methods: Observational video-coded subset of 250 attacking-player/defender collisions with defender falling, from a parent set of 501 incidents in the 2009/10 Israeli Basketball Super League; chi-square comparisons plus logistic regression for home status, player reputation, team reputation and relative size.
N1  - Findings: 48 of 250 incidents (19.2%) produced an offensive-foul call. Home/away chi-square p=.902; team-status p=.912; size p=.410; logistic regression corroborated no statistically significant predictor among tested factors. Authors explicitly say rarity and small sample made it challenging to look for sequential effects.
N1  - Limits: Offensive fouls with defender falls are a narrow event class, and this is Israeli league evidence rather than NBA defensive-foul evidence. Only 250 selected incidents and uneven groups (e.g., 15 defending-player-favorite cases) limit precision; nonsignificance does not prove no bias. The current analysis concerns call patterns and contextual proxies, not comprehensive independent correctness labeling or a causal identification design.
N1  - Evidence: PDF p. 3: sample, coding and procedure; PDF pp. 4–5: chi-square comparisons and logistic regression Table 6; PDF pp. 5–6: sample and sequential-effect limitations
