N1  - Sors and colleagues manipulate crowd sound while 30 basketball referees classify expert-labeled contact videos and measure competitive anxiety. Pressing sound reduces discrimination in the high-anxiety group, while the overall sound effect is nonsignificant, showing that contextual effects can differ across officials. I will use this study as a possible mechanism and limitation rather than evidence that WMI directly detects crowd pressure or referee bias.
N1  - Methods: Laboratory repeated-measures calm versus pressing crowd audio, with 30 male active referees (Italian youth leagues through national second division), median-split into 15 low/15 high competitive-anxiety participants; 20 expert-consensus clips each paired with both sounds, 40 trials; signal-detection d-prime analyzed with 2×2 mixed ANOVA.
N1  - Findings: No main effect of crowd sound (p=.97) or anxiety (p=.69), but interaction F(1,28)=7.33, p<.05, partial eta-squared=.21. High-anxiety referees had lower discrimination under pressing sound (d-prime 1.04 calm versus .73 pressing; paired t(14)=2.24, p<.05, d=.64). Low-anxiety within-participant difference was nonsignificant (p=.11); the study did not identify home/away teams to participants.
N1  - Limits: Small, all-male non-NBA sample; median split is sample-dependent rather than a validated anxiety cutoff. Video/headphone task and two particular sound tracks limit ecological generalization; tracks differ qualitatively and quantitatively. Experimental sound manipulation supports a task-specific effect but does not establish that NBA foul sequences are caused by crowd pressure.
N1  - Evidence: PDF pp. 2–3: participants, stimuli and procedure; PDF p. 4: signal-detection analysis and results; PDF pp. 5–6: generalizability and implications
