N1  - Cervone and colleagues model basketball possessions with continuous player movements and discrete transitions to estimate expected possession value from information available at each moment. Their hierarchical model improves held-out transition likelihood over simpler alternatives, but the analysis explicitly excludes possessions with fouls. I will use its conditional-information framework and model-comparison logic to design WMI prediction tests while identifying foul modeling as an important difference.
N1  - Methods: Multiresolution stochastic process for 25 Hz NBA optical tracking from 2013–14; micro-level player motion combined with macro-level passes, shots, turnovers and possession-end states, with hierarchical spatial sharing across 461 ballcarriers. Fits 90% of data and compares held-out transition log likelihood on the remaining 10%.
N1  - Findings: Full hierarchical model outperformed the simpler models for every transition class in Table 1; e.g., shot attempt held-out log likelihood improved from -48.9 thousand (player-only) to -40.7 thousand (full). Spatial effects without hierarchical shrinkage could hurt prediction; pass4 model without shrinkage scored -24.5 thousand versus -20.4 thousand player-only and -18.9 thousand full. Illustrates EPV paths and derived player summaries, rather than a foul-history effect.
N1  - Limits: Explicitly excludes possessions containing fouls because foul type/circumstances are insufficiently annotated. Semi-Markov coarsening can miss coordinated multi-action plays; rebounds simplified to constant team probability and turnover types aggregated. Tracking still omits limb positions and other relevant information; computation and proprietary data access constrain replication.
N1  - Evidence: Analyzed version: arXiv:1408.0777v3, February 25, 2016; PDF p. 5 (printed p. 4): EPV definition, foul exclusion and footnote; PDF pp. 19–20 (printed pp. 18–19): 90/10 split and Table 1; PDF pp. 24–25 (printed pp. 23–24): discussion and model assumptions
