KW  - WMI Checkpoint 3
N1  - Sicilia and colleagues use an LSTM and player embeddings to estimate possession terminal-action probabilities, including shooting and nonshooting fouls, from optical tracking windows. DeepHoops beats a frequency baseline on reported Brier scores and illustrates micro-action valuation, although downsampling and the preprint evaluation design limit direct comparison to WMI. I will use its outcome definitions, pre-event cutoff, and probability-evaluation ideas to test whether past fouls add information beyond game context.
N1  - Methods: Tracking from 750 NBA 2016–17 games and over 134,000 possessions; stacked three-layer LSTM with learned player embeddings; roughly five-second windows with a 16-frame (~.64-second) blind spot before terminal action. Classifies shot, shooting foul, nonshooting foul, turnover, or null; downsamples null class with K sampled windows per possession; 75/10/15 training/validation/test split.
N1  - Findings: At K=2, multiclass Brier score .3598 versus climatology .4920 and Brier skill score .2686; authors report best relative skill among K=1–4. Reliability diagrams broadly track predicted probabilities, including shooting/nonshooting foul labels; tail bin for turnovers contains just one estimate. For 512 assists versus 512 randomly sampled passes, over 72% of assists increased estimated points; distributions differed at .1% level.
N1  - Limits: This PDF is a working preprint with a placeholder DOI; cite arXiv rather than the placeholder. Evaluation uses a downsampling scheme with altered class prevalence; the reported scores cannot be transferred directly to natural-prevalence WMI possession predictions. Method does not clearly document a chronological game-grouped split; do not infer proof of future-season generalization. League-average terminal-action point values omit lineup-specific payoff; micro-action before/after valuation is not a randomized causal effect.
N1  - Evidence: Analyzed version: arXiv:1902.08081v1, February 21, 2019; ignore manuscript placeholder DOI; PDF pp. 6–7: 750 games, windows, labels and blind spot; PDF pp. 11–13: evaluation split, Brier scores and reliability; PDF pp. 13–15: pass comparison, examples and discussion
