N1  - This study compared expert judgments with 283 deliberately difficult basketball officiating events and found that calls outside the responsible referee’s coverage area were associated with more errors. Referee rank and years of experience did not independently explain accuracy, while the selected ambiguous sample prevents treating its error rate as a league-wide estimate. It will support the paper’s discussion of unobserved positioning and crew coordination and the need to distinguish foul frequency from decision correctness.
N1  - Methods: Observational coding of 321 deliberately ambiguous events from 100 Israeli Super League games in 2016–17. Nine elite international referee observers assessed edited clips; two independently assessed each event with a third for disagreement. Excluding 38 cases without consensus left 283 events involving 25 on-court referees (18 national, 7 international). Chi-square, ANOVA and binomial logistic regression related accuracy to primary coverage responsibility, rank, experience and distance.
N1  - Findings: Only 109/283 decisions (38.5%) matched expert assessments, reflecting selection of difficult cases, not a representative league accuracy rate. Errors occurred in 49/60 events (81.7%) with incorrect teamwork/coverage responsibility versus 125/223 (56.1%) with correct responsibility (Table4). Table6 reports teamwork as the only statistically significant predictor among teamwork, rank, experience and distance; rank and experience were not significantly associated with accuracy.
N1  - Limits: Purposive selection of contentious plays inflates errors relative to ordinary play; exclusion of unresolved expert disagreement may further affect the sample. Exploratory single-league observational data cannot establish that position causes accuracy; no referee gaze measures were available. Authors explicitly call for validation in NBA and EuroLeague.
N1  - Evidence: PDF pp. 3–4 (printed 315–316): Methods, Database, Procedures, Statistical Analyses; PDF pp. 5–8 (printed 317–320): Results and Tables 4, 6, 7; PDF pp. 10–11 (printed 322–323): Discussion; Limitations and Future Research
