N1  - Using Spanish women’s basketball records, the authors found no consistent evidence that Black or foreign players received more fouls after adjustment or that all-male crews differed from mixed crews. Foreign players received fewer fouls in games ending within three points, although the study could not directly measure stress or foul correctness. This source will help justify contextual controls and careful interpretation of null findings while highlighting why final-game information must be excluded from a live prediction model.
N1  - Methods: Official statistics for Spanish women’s first and second divisions, 2014–15 to 2018–19: 2,555 matches, 58 teams, 53,398 player-match records. Main homogeneous two-referee analysis drops first-division 2018–19, with full controlled models using 47,258 observations. Linear regressions use fouls per 40 minutes and a fifth-foul indicator, sequential player and match controls and team, season and referee fixed effects; standard errors cluster at player level. Close-game subsample is defined by final margin ≤3 points.
N1  - Findings: No significant adverse association with Black identity or foreign nationality in full controlled sample; mixed versus all-male referee composition also lacked significant effects. In close games, foreign players had fewer fouls per 40 minutes (fully controlled coefficient −0.789, SE0.392, p<.05; Table3); this is an observational association. No significant nationality or race effect on fifth fouls among players finishing with four or five; player position and performance controls matter for foul rates.
N1  - Limits: Final margin is an imperfect stress proxy and is future information relative to an in-game prediction; attendance data were unavailable. Race was inferred from photos; all-female referee teams were too rare for strong subgroup conclusions. Calls do not reveal whether contact warranted a foul, and observational null results are not proof that all bias is absent.
N1  - Evidence: PDF pp. 3–5: Data and Methods; Empirical Strategy; PDF pp. 5–7: Tables 2–4 and Results; PDF pp. 8–9: Discussion and limitations
