N1  - Using 18,182 CBA foul records, this study found that call allocation varied with score and accumulated foul differentials, including fewer subsequent calls on teams already carrying more fouls. Its observational, call-only sample cannot separate tactical contact from referee decisions or estimate the chance of a foul on an arbitrary possession. It will motivate contextual controls and a clear distinction between predicting whether a foul occurs and studying which team receives a recorded call.
N1  - Methods: Event study of 2022–23 CBA official records:18,519 foul events in441games, final18,182calls in439games. Technical/disqualifying fouls, overtime and specified outliers excluded. Independent t tests and binary logistic regressions study which team received a recorded foul (home/away, leading/trailing, higher/lower foul counts), with score differential, foul differentials, quarter, team-strength categories, format and game type. Models are explicitly explanatory, not predictive.
N1  - Findings: Higher existing foul counts were associated with relatively fewer additional calls, consistent with balancing at the recorded-call level. Quarter-level more-versus-fewer model reports quarter foul differential OR0.893 and score differential OR1.017 (p<.05). Home-versus-away model has format OR0.913, score differential OR1.019, and quarter foul differential OR0.913 (p<.05); aggregate format comparisons were not significant. Game-level more-versus-fewer model had poor Hosmer–Lemeshow fit; quarter-level model fit was acceptable.
N1  - Limits: Called events alone omit non-call possessions and actual opportunities; cannot identify probability of any foul next possession or call correctness. Single season and coexistence of COVID venue formats limit generalization; strategic behavior, player/bench interactions and psychological factors are unobserved. Whole-season win percentage used for strength would leak future information in prospective WMI prediction. Repeated-event dependence and the exact pre/post-call timing of foul differential need care before adapting the model.
