N1  - This review synthesized twelve studies of sports officials and found mixed relationships between expertise, gaze patterns, and performance. Most studies used artificial viewing conditions, none examined basketball, and the authors considered evidence insufficient for firm training recommendations. It will support the paper’s methodological caution that possession-level patterns cannot by themselves identify referee perception, psychological momentum, or incorrect calls.
N1  - Methods: Review searched EBSCO Discovery Service and Google Scholar in March2020, English peer-reviewed studies measuring actual gaze variables plus reference scanning. Twelve studies: five association football, three gymnastics, one each softball, handball, rugby and ice hockey; none basketball. Narrative comparison rather than pooled meta-analysis.
N1  - Findings: Seven studies reported expertise-related gaze differences while four did not; five reported both gaze and performance differences, and four performance differences without gaze differences. Only three studies measured gaze in real play, and only one examined a referee team; evidence on peripheral vision and shared gaze was scarce. Authors conclude evidence is too limited and contradictory for confident gaze-training recommendations.
N1  - Limits: Small, heterogeneous evidence base with varying expertise definitions, tasks, eye trackers and sample sizes; many studies lack power or multiple-comparison discussion. No included basketball study, so application to NBA is indirect and later work must be considered. Gaze alone does not capture stress, fatigue, anticipation, interpretation or decision correctness.
